  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1124 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nRain chances return to the region by Saturday morning as a \nprogressive shortwave trough transits just to our south. This should \nbring us a round of scattered light showers through the first half \nof the day, before a second round of heavier activity associated \nwith a more potent trough arrives Saturday evening. Large-scale \nforcing for ascent will be relatively strong which could result in \nsome locally heavy rainfall especially off the Caprock, but we still \ndo not anticipate any flooding impacts given the overall quick-\nhitting nature of the system. Current forecast rainfall totals are \nlowest on the Caprock (less than a half inch), and are highest over \nthe Rolling Plains where a few locations could pick up close to an \ninch by midday Sunday. Cloud cover and on-and-off showers will \nresult in a relatively cool weekend with highs both Saturday and \nSunday likely remaining in the 50s area-wide. The system will exit \nto our east by Sunday night, which will in turn allow a reinforcing \ncold front to pass through the region. Monday and Tuesday will \ntherefore be the chilliest days of the upcoming week, with \npersistent low cloud cover likely to keep temperatures below 50 \ndegrees each day. A slight warmup then arrives for the second half \nof the week, with rain chances returning Thursday and beyond.",
